Summary

This PR documents a critical business logic vulnerability in the Bridge.receiveTokens flow that allows non-canonical bytes32 recipient values to bypass validation and result in irreversible fund loss.

The issue arises from accepting arbitrary bytes32 values as recipient addresses and later truncating them to address(uint160(...)), which silently discards high bits. This allows malformed inputs that pass validation but resolve to unintended addresses at execution time.
Impact

Funds can be irreversibly burned or misdirected

Affected paths include native coin withdrawals and ERC-20 mint/transfer flows

Vulnerability is exploitable by a relayer role with valid permissions

Issue is not theoretical — a reproducible PoC demonstrates the behavior on-chain

Root Cause

receiveTokensParams.toAddress is validated only as bytes32 != 0

Later cast using:

address(uint160(uint256(receiveTokensParams.toAddress)))


High-bit contamination is not checked or rejected

Non-canonical bytes32 values truncate silently during address conversion

Proof of Concept

A Foundry-based test demonstrates:

A poisoned bytes32 recipient value

Successful validation

Silent truncation to a canonical address

Execution resulting in fund loss

